DAY 01 — OSINT & PASSIVE RECONNAISSANCE
Python-based passive intelligence collection lab
1. Objective
Understand OSINT and passive reconnaissance, then implement a Python scanner that collects publicly available WHOIS, DNS,
IP-resolution, geolocation, indexed host intelligence, and passive subdomain information without intentionally probing target
infrastructure.
2. Lab Environment
Parrot OS VM; Python 3; requests; dnspython; python-whois; jq 1.7; JSON/TXT output.
3. Methodology
The scanner normalizes the supplied hostname, performs WHOIS and recursive DNS lookups, resolves IPv4/IPv6, queries
third-party geolocation and Shodan InternetDB services, and performs passive subdomain discovery through crt.sh, HackerTarget,
and AlienVault OTX. Independent lookups run concurrently and provider failures are captured.
4. Implementation Review
The scanner is modular, uses ThreadPoolExecutor for independent lookups, handles HTTP/provider failures without terminating the
complete scan, and exports structured JSON plus subdomains.txt. Terminology was revised to avoid absolute claims such as
“100% passive” and “zero digital footprint.”
5. Observed Results — Private Lab Evidence
The final run resolved scanme.nmap.org to 45.33.32.156. WHOIS returned dates and nameservers but a null registrar field. DNS
returned an A record and SPF-related TXT data; no AAAA, MX, or NS answer was returned for the queried hostname. IP
geolocation associated the address with Falkenstein, Saxony, Germany and Hetzner Online GmbH. Shodan InternetDB returned
indexed ports, hostnames, CPEs, and two CVE identifiers. Passive subdomain discovery returned one unique result while two
providers failed (HTTP 404 and timeout).
6. Key Analytical Lessons
A DNS A record proves a hostname-to-IPv4 mapping, not current availability. Indexed ports are observations, not live verification.
Port numbers do not prove protocols. IP geolocation describes approximate infrastructure location, not necessarily organizational
location. Null WHOIS fields do not prove that information is nonexistent. OSINT analysis should distinguish observation from
interpretation and conclusion.
7. Scope, Ethics & Publication
No intentional direct probing of the target IP or discovered services was performed. Raw reconnaissance data containing real
infrastructure details should remain private. Public GitHub artifacts should use sanitized sample data and exclude unnecessary
third-party infrastructure details.
8. Validation
jq successfully parsed scan.json. python3 -m py_compile osint_scanner.py completed without syntax errors. The revised scanner
source also passed syntax validation.
9. Conclusion
Day 01 objectives were completed. The project demonstrates passive OSINT collection, third-party intelligence aggregation,
structured output, concurrency, error handling, and careful interpretation of incomplete or indexed evidence.
Day 01 Status: COMPLETE
